# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: dwg-round-trip-loses-entities — a DWG written by clautolisp comes back with no entities
#+STATUS: DONE
#+PRIORITY: P1
#+TYPE: drawing-codec
#+DEPENDS: -
#+BRANCH: -
#+DATE: 2026-09-26

* Observed

Once a DWG could be written at all
(dwg-write-rejects-a-drawing-built-in-memory, fixed in 2.2.101), the file
turns out to carry no entities:

#+begin_example
;; a drawing PARSED from a DWG, plus one LINE
parsed source entities: 0
parsed + 1 entity:      1
parsed round trip:      0        <- the LINE is gone

;; the DWG an ActiveX SaveAs produced from a session with a LINE
activex-written dwg: entities 0
#+end_example

It is not about being built in memory: a drawing read from
=autolisp-front-end/source/empty.dwg=, given one entity, written as DWG
and read back, loses it too. The write reports success (libredwg rc 0)
and the file is ~300 KB.

A geometry-losing save that reports success is as bad as it sounds: a
user's drawing is silently emptied.

* Where to look

=dwg-write-drawing= (clautolisp/drawing-dwg/source/codec.lisp) writes a
temp DXF with =dxf-write-drawing= and hands it to libredwg's
=clal_dxf_to_dwg=. Two candidates, and the temp DXF settles which:

1. *Our DXF's ENTITIES are not acceptable to libredwg's DXF reader.* Most
   likely the OWNERSHIP: in a DXF a real entity carries its owner block
   record (group 330) and the entities of model space belong to
   =*Model_Space=, whose BLOCK definition we do not even emit (the BLOCKS
   section of a cador document has zero BLOCK entries although the
   BLOCK_RECORD table names both spaces). libredwg may parse the entity
   and then drop it for want of an owner.
2. *The READ side loses them.* =dwg-read-drawing= converts DWG to DXF and
   parses that. Run =clal_dwg_to_dxf= by hand on a written file and look
   for the LINE in the DXF it produces: present means our DXF reader
   drops it, absent means the write did.

Keep the temp DXF (or write it explicitly with
=(write-drawing d "x.dxf" :format :dxf-ascii)=) and compare its ENTITIES
section with the one libredwg emits for a real DWG that has a line --
the same diff-the-two-files method that found the two header defects.

* Note

=dwg-write-then-read-round-trips= has been green throughout: it asserts
the format and the tables, not the entities. That is the gap that let
this stand. The new
=dwg-writes-a-drawing-built-in-memory= documents today's behaviour with
an explicit "entity count is zero" assertion pointing here, so fixing
this issue turns that assertion around.

* Acceptance criteria

- A drawing with entities written as DWG reads back with those entities,
  from an in-memory drawing and from a parsed one.
- Both round-trip tests assert entity counts and identities, not just the
  format.
- The BLOCKS section carries the =*Model_Space= / =*Paper_Space= block
  definitions the BLOCK_RECORD table declares, if that turns out to be
  the missing piece.
- The SCHME+ A1 DWG scenario sees its geometry after a save.

* Progress — clautolisp 2.2.103: the owner is written; a bare skeleton is
  still not enough

The loss is on the WRITE side, settled first: our DXF contains the LINE,
the DXF libredwg regenerates from the DWG it built does not. So libredwg's
DXF reader drops it, and the question was what it wants.

** What libredwg requires, measured

Written as file-surgery variants converted through the shim, and read back:

| the entity's group 330 (owner)                    | entities back |
|---------------------------------------------------+---------------|
| absent                                            |             0 |
| absent, but with 100 AcDbEntity / AcDbLine        |             0 |
| present and RESOLVING to the *Model_Space record  |             1 |
| present but pointing at a handle nothing declares |             0 |

So the OWNER is the requirement and the subclass markers are irrelevant.
And it is necessary, not sufficient: with a resolving owner but our own
minimal BLOCKS section the entity is still dropped, while with a resolving
owner and the BLOCKS section of a real drawing it survives.

** Fixed

- =dxf-entity-data-with-owner= writes =(330 . H)= for every entity that
  does not carry an owner of its own, H being the handle of the block
  record it belongs to -- its block, or =*Model_Space= for a model-space
  entity. An entity that already has an owner keeps it.
- =dxf-owner-handle= allocates a handle for a record that has none (a DXF
  handle is not optional; AutoCAD's writer assigns them too) and inserts
  it AFTER the record's =(0 . type)= pair, because a DXF object must begin
  with its type -- putting it first emitted =5 <h>= before
  =0 BLOCK_RECORD= and broke the table.
- =dxf-complete-block-header= gives each block header its own handle, its
  record as owner, and the AcDbEntity / AcDbBlockBegin markers.
- =dxf-ensure-write-handles= does both BEFORE any section is written:
  TABLES precedes ENTITIES and BLOCKS on the stream, so a handle
  allocated while writing an entity would have named a record that had
  already gone out without it.

Result: *a drawing whose tables and blocks come from a real DWG now keeps
its entities through a DWG round trip* — the read-modify-write case.
Test: =dwg-parsed-drawing-keeps-its-entities=, which compares the same
drawing round-tripped with and without one added entity, since libredwg's
own writer is lossy on rich samples (example_2000.dwg: 228 entities in,
183 out, and an entity added to it is lost among them; sample_2000.dwg,
example_r13 and example_r14 are lossless, which is why the fixture is
sample_2000.dwg). Plus =dxf-entities-carry-their-owner-block-record= and
=dxf-entities-keep-an-owner-they-already-have= in clautolisp/drawing, with
no native library needed. Suites: clautolisp 10017 checks, drawing-dwg
25 checks, 0 failures.

** What remains: a drawing built from NOTHING

A drawing assembled in this process -- which is what a cador document is,
and what the binary's ActiveX SaveAs writes -- still comes back empty, and
not for want of the owner: the record and the block header now carry
everything the reference file has. Ruled out one at a time, each measured:
the subclass markers and 70/280/281 flags on the block record, its owner
330, the TABLE header shape (identical in both), the OBJECTS section
(removing it from a working file keeps the entity), the HEADER variables
(transplanting the rich header does not help), and the LAYER record's
identity. Transplanting the reference TABLES *and* BLOCKS together does
work, so what is missing is somewhere in the breadth of a real table set,
not in any single record we have tried.

*The practical way out, already measured:* build a new drawing FROM THE
BUNDLED TEMPLATE instead of synthesising a skeleton.
=autolisp-front-end/source/empty.dwg= exists for exactly this, and
reading it, adding a LINE and writing DWG gives the entity back (1 of 1).
A cador document created from that template would inherit a complete,
real structure -- tables, blocks, dictionaries -- and its saves would keep
their geometry. That is a smaller and more honest change than growing a
hand-written skeleton that must then be maintained against libredwg's and
AutoCAD's expectations.

=dwg-writes-a-drawing-built-in-memory= documents today's zero count and
points here, so the assertion turns around when this is finished.

* Progress — clautolisp 2.2.106: the template exists and works; wiring
  cador to it is a model decision

The way out measured above now ships as an API, with the template itself
committed:

- =clautolisp/drawing/template/empty-drawing.dxf= — 37 KB of DXF produced
  from the bundled empty drawing: 249 header variables, the real symbol
  table records, and the =*Model_Space= / =*Paper_Space= block definitions.
  It is a =:static-file= of =clautolisp/drawing=, so it installs with the
  system.
- =make-drawing-from-template= reads it and overrides name / path / format
  / version. Being DXF, it needs NO native library -- the DXF codec is
  pure Lisp -- and it falls back to =make-drawing= when the template
  cannot be read, so nothing breaks if a deployment lacks it (such a
  drawing then loses entities through a DWG round trip, which is the
  state before all this).
- =make-drawing= is unchanged and remains right for a LOADER, which is
  about to fill the drawing from a file.

Test: =dwg-a-drawing-from-the-template-keeps-its-entities= creates a
drawing from the template, adds a LINE, writes DWG and reads it back with
the entity. drawing-dwg suite 30 checks, clautolisp 10017 checks, 0
failures.

** The decision left: does a cador document come from the template?

Wiring cador's two creation sites to =make-drawing-from-template= is one
line each, and it was tried: it makes the DWG a session saves keep its
geometry, which is what the SCHME+ A1 scenario needs. It also makes a NEW
DOCUMENT NON-EMPTY, and 22 cador tests assert today's empty one:

#+begin_example
EMPTY-CADOR-HAS-EMPTY-HANDLE-TABLES        TBLNEXT-WALKS-AND-REWINDS
ENTLAST-AND-ENTNEXT-WALK-CREATION-ORDER    BLOCKS-ADD-CREATES-A-BLOCK-…
POPULATE-DEFAULT-SYSVARS-FULL-INSTALLS-1836 (1880 with the template)
DOCUMENT-BLOCKS-IS-A-LIVE-COLLECTION-WITH-LAYOUT-BLOCKS   …
#+end_example

Every one of those is an assumption about what a fresh document contains,
not a defect in the change -- a real CAD's new drawing does carry all of
it, and =tblnext=, =entget= and the Blocks collection would see it. That
is a deliberate change to the document model, with a visible surface, so
it wants pjb's decision rather than 22 test edits made in passing. The
change itself is ready: revert-free, one line per site.

One fix from that attempt is kept because it stands alone:
=populate-default-tables= now FILLS A GAP instead of overwriting, so a
drawing that already carries a record keeps it, and the seeded records'
type marker is =BLOCK_RECORD= with the DXF underscore rather than
=BLOCK-RECORD= from the keyword name.

* Resolved — clautolisp 2.2.121: the template is a SYSVAR, so nothing is imposed

The decision this was parked on — "does a cador document come from the
template?" — was a false dilemma, and pjb named the way out (2026-09-26): /"Il
doit y avoir une sysvar pour specifier un template non?"/ A system variable makes
the structure an explicit, per-session, scriptable CHOICE instead of a silent
change to what "new document" means. Both answers coexist:

- =CLAUTOLISPNEWDRAWINGTEMPLATE= (string, default ""): the drawing a NEW document
  is created from. EMPTY keeps today's empty document, so the 22 cador tests that
  assert it stay correct — they were never wrong, they were describing the
  default.
- The ENVIRONMENT variable of the same name governs the STARTUP document, which
  is built in an =:initform= before any AutoLISP can run. That is why both exist.
- A RELATIVE name resolves against BricsCAD's =TEMPLATEPATH= (the Templates
  folder), already in the vendor catalogue, so a script can name just the file
  the way it would in BricsCAD.
- An unreadable value is reported on =*error-output*= and the document is created
  empty: a typo in a variable neither ends the session nor passes unnoticed.

Measured end to end on the BUILT binary — a fresh session, a LINE added through
ActiveX, saved as DWG, read back with the drawing API:

#+begin_example
$ CLAUTOLISPNEWDRAWINGTEMPLATE=.../drawing/template/empty-drawing.dxf \
  clautolisp -norc -q -l probe.lsp          # vla-addline + vla-saveas
SAVED
$ ... read it back ...
ENTITIES=1 BLOCKS=1
#+end_example

Without the variable the same probe yields =ENTITIES=0=: that IS this defect, and
it is now a configuration away rather than a wall.

Implementation: =CADOR-NEW-DRAWING-TEMPLATE= / =CADOR-MAKE-NEW-DRAWING=
(cador/source/sysvars.lisp) for documents opened during a session;
=%STARTUP-DRAWING= (model.lisp, where the two sysvar NAMES also live, because
that file loads first and one definition beats the same string written twice) for
the startup one; and =CLAUTOLISP.DRAWING:MAKE-DRAWING-FROM-TEMPLATE= gained a
=:TEMPLATE= argument — it had always read the bundled one — and now SNIFFS the
template's format instead of assuming DXF, since a user's template is as likely
to be a .dwg or a .dwt.

Tests: five in cador — the default empty document, which PINS the assumption the
other 22 rest on and says so in its docstring; the sysvar's template used; an
unreadable value warning and staying empty; a relative name resolved against
TEMPLATEPATH; and the startup document following the environment, through an
injected template argument so no environment variable has to be set in-process —
plus the existing drawing-dwg round trip. Whole clautolisp suite green on SBCL
(34 suites, 0 failures).

Documented in the user manual twice, as AGENTS.md requires of a user-visible
setting: /The ActiveX / COM object model/ (what it does, and why DWG needs it)
and /Environment variables/ (the startup knob). RELEASE_NOTES.org too.

** One question left for pjb, not decided here

AutoCAD's own name for this is, from memory, =QNEWTEMPLATE= — and it is in
NEITHER autolisp-spec NOR =system-variables-inventory.sexp=, which was generated
from help.autodesk.com 2026. Adding a vendor sysvar to the normative
specification on a recollection is precisely what AGENTS.md forbids, so it was
not done. Filed as a probe question in =deferred-spec-research.issue=: if AutoCAD
2026 does document QNEWTEMPLATE, accepting it as a second spelling here is a
two-line change, plus a spec entry and a document-version bump.
